manages and reflects on his/her/their individual role in accordance with his/her personal development plan;
manages and reflects on his/her/their activities within the project team, in accordance with the project organization;
Evaluates the designed solution with the stakeholders (client and supervisor)
Problem analysis and definition
The student:
sets up and documents an appropriate project organization with co-workers such that the project team is leading and the supervisor has an advising role;
analyses a given problem and formulates this problem into a clear problem statement and project goal;
investigates various systematic approaches and motivates the selection of the most suited approach;
elaborates the selected approach into a project plan including scope, approach and planning in accordance with the stakeholders (client and supervisor);
Design and Engineering
The student:
analyses the technical aspects of the problem along with conducting a literature research and documents these findings;
designs a concept solution for a technical problem based on the findings of the analyses using a systematic approach in close cooperation with the client;
engineers the designed solution such that the design is ready to be implemented;
documents and motivates the designed solution to the stakeholders;

/en/course/2026/WBVB25MB.outcomes:
The student can:
Apply the machinery directive in the process of designing a safe machine. Moreover, the student understands the various subjects of the directive and knows how/where to use them;
Conduct a risk-analysis on a machine;
Indicate which design principles are to be used during the design of a construction based on stiffness and make a substantiated choice of the optimum shape of the construction in a given situation;
Specify a number of machine parts (brakes, clutches, gears, etc.) based on applicable demands and by comparing different options provided by suppliers;
Can analyze and solve practical dynamics problems by first correctly schematizing the situation using free‑body diagrams (FBDs) and kinetic diagrams (KDs), after which the corresponding calculations are performed
Provide and solve equations for impulse and momentum in a practical situation;
Size machine parts and sub-assemblies based on strength and stiffness calculations using FEM-software and based on worst case scenarios encountered during the actual application of the machine. The design calculations are properly validated and documented.

/en/course/2026/WBVB25AFS.content:
During the graduation project, the student demonstrates that he/she has acquired all competencies and skills characteristic of a mechanical engineer.
The student:
ndependently analyzes a complex and unstructured technical problem within a multidisciplinary context and understands the current situation and the client’s interests, based on which he/she formulates the objectives and requirements of the problem;
independently designs a solution to the problem based on the requirements, taking into account societal interests as well as the feasibility and testability of the solution by applying appropriate design methods;
independently realizes and implements a complex product or process, which he/she designs to demonstrate that the correct materials and methods have been used and to verify that the product/process meets the requirements. The student correctly documents the analysis, design, and realization;
demonstrates an understanding of the maintenance aspects of the product/process by developing implementation and maintenance plans and monitoring the performance of the product/process;
plans the project based on the available resources, adjusts it where necessary, and communicates appropriately with stakeholders;
formulates appropriate recommendations based on research and an up-to-date overview of the professional field and presents these recommendations clearly to those involved. He/she provides advice that aligns with the stated question;
formulates the research question based on the objectives and requirements of the project, derived from the problem analysis. In this analysis, the student investigates the context and possible solutions using literature or other information sources and presents the results and conclusions of this research in a structured manner, while remaining critical of the research method and results;
behaves professionally in a complex environment by reflecting on his/her own behavior, giving and receiving constructive feedback, and communicating effectively in English or Dutch. In doing so, the student fully documents his/her research and all phases/stages of the project and highlights the key aspects and results in a presentation.

/en/course/2026/WBVB24PDI.outcomes:
The student:
describes what the total product lifecycle for a given product looks like, from design, production plan, production organization, production and delivery;
analyzes the influence of a product's design choices on manufacturing technology, cost price, production planning, lead time and factory design;
draws up a work preparation in which production steps, times and costs are specified;
draws up a production plan including lead times and critical path for a given product;
describes the manufacturing process through systems and models
determines the cost price of a product from material and production costs, calculates a simple hourly rate for people and machines as a basis for the cost price of a product (part);

/en/course/2026/WBVB24DRE.content:
Various mechanical devices use moving parts to perform their functions. Product development then requires insight into kinetics, strength, stiffness, stability and specifying/selecting suitable machine parts. During this module, topics range from:
modelling and quantifying loads, accelerations and velocities;
energy/power requirements;
Strength, stiffness and stability analyses;
selecting AC motors with appropriate gear ratios.

/en/course/2026/WBVB26MDC-EN.outcomes:
After finishing this module the student can:
Operationalizes the performance dimensions of a given process;
Describe the information flow and the production planning of a production organization;
Makes a correct process map, calculates a correct economic order quantity, creates a correct MRP-schedule
Recognizes process improvement methods, applies Lean Manufacturing methods
Describe the data and document management process, the role of CAD / CAM / CAE in the design and production process;
Describe the role and functionalities of PLM/PDM, ERP systems within a company;
Apply product and data management in PLM software (Windchill);
Describe the importance, trends and elements of Industry 4.0 – Industrial Internet of Things;

/en/course/2026/MZVP24PD1.content:
You will receive lessons on artistic/professional identity, entrepreneurship, networking, business, and project-based (collaboration) work. In this course you will work on:
- collecting feedback on your actions, the work you make and analyzing the collected feedback;
- exploring your artistic identity, ambitions, network and way of working.

/en/course/2026/MZVP24PD2.content:
You will receive lessons on artistic/professional identity, entrepreneurship, networking, business, and project-based (collaboration). In this course you will work on:
- developing a personal ‘business plan’ (growth model);
- the relationship and interaction between your work and the environment.

/en/course/2026/MZVB26PD6.content:
You will receive lessons on entrepreneurship, presentation, organization and you will work on projects. In doing so, you will develop knowledge of group dynamics and creative processes and apply this in professional situations. In this course, you will work on:
- broadening and/or deepening your artistic/educational practice and expanding your business portfolio;
- recognizing group dynamics and aligning your own actions with them;
- the interaction between creative group processes and your own artistic vision and identity.
You do this by working under guidance on your own project, in which you bring your own product or practice to the market.
In addition, this semester includes Group Dynamics classes in which you design and test creative musical workshops.

/en/course/2026/MZVB25GP8.content:
The graduation program consists of your own project in which you demonstrate the learning outcomes of the program at the final level using a question from practice. You can come up with this question yourself or it can be a question from a client: A festival, stage, arts center, band, choir, orchestra, advertising agency, artist collective, you name it! The question must challenge you to show who you are as a professional musician and how you position yourself in the field in which you are or want to be active. You can come up with and/or execute the project alone or together with other students. You carry out the graduation project independently and are coached (intervision) by supervisors of the program. Individual guidance takes place through consultation.
In the graduation project you work on the following content:
- applying a broad and up-to-date range of (music) professional knowledge and skills in a complex professional situation or context;
- giving shape to musical expressions based on your own artistic vision and identity;
- signalling developments in professional practice and society and positioning yourself and your own work in relation to these;
- evaluating your own and/or other people's actions and musical/artistic expressions on the basis of reflection and research;
- applying appropriate communication and social skills in complex professional situations or contexts;
- independently and sustainably designing professional situations and networks in which you realise your ambitions.
